<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mubarak al-Sabah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1837">1837</a>–<a href="/years/1915">1915</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mubarak the Great · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-sabah">Al Sabah</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mubarak al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a>, called Mubarak the Great, was ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a> from 1896 to 1915. He took power by killing his half-brothers, and in 1899 signed a secret agreement placing Kuwait under British protection against Ottoman claims. He intervened in central Arabian politics, sheltering the exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">Al Saud</a>, and his descendants have ruled Kuwait ever since.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mubarak-al-sabah.jpg" alt="Mubarak al-Sabah" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hermann Burchardt (d. 1909) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mubarak_bin_Sabah_as-Sabah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mubarak was born into the al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a> family in 1837, a son of Sheikh Sabah II Al-Sabah, who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a> from 1859 to 1866. As a grown man he served mainly as cavalry commander of Kuwait's forces, including in several Ottoman campaigns, most notably those of 1871, 1892 and 1894 into Hasa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatar">Qatar</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. In August 1879 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> gave him the rank of Grand Equerry for a campaign into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatif">Qatif</a> and southern Iraq. He met Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lewis-pelly">Lewis Pelly</a>, the British political resident in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, in 1863, and in 1883 he went on an Ottoman diplomatic mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His half-brothers Muhammad and Jarrah were killed on 8 May 1896. Accounts of the killing vary: Jill Crystal holds that Mubarak, his sons Jabir and Salim and loyal supporters killed them in secret during the night. Mubarak then spent his late brother's wealth on gifts to the Ottoman bureaucracy to win recognition. The Ottoman Council hesitated, and Hamdi Pasha, the Wali of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, proposed military intervention, but in December 1897 Mubarak was named kaymakam, or sub-governor, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a>, since force threatened to destabilise the region further.</p><p>Under British protection he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a> in 1901 against the Rashidi, but most of his army was destroyed at the Battle of Sarif on 17 March 1901, his brother and two nephews among the dead. After a stand-off between an Ottoman and a British warship, the Status Quo Agreement of September 1901 barred troops of either power from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a> and left Ottoman authority nominal. In October 1907 Mubarak sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the rights to any railway terminus, for £4000 a year, blocking the German-Ottoman plan to bring the Berlin-Baghdad Railway to the port. The Anglo-Ottoman Convention of 1913 recognised Kuwait as an autonomous kaza of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> began Mubarak sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. In November 1914 he sent a force to expel the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> from Umm Qasr, Safwan, Bubiyan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, and Britain recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a> as an independent government under British protection. He removed the Ottoman symbol from the Kuwaiti flag and put the word Kuwait in Arabic script in its place. Weakened by illness in his last years, he died in November 1915 of malaria aggravated by a bad heart. His sons Jaber and Salim succeeded him, and the constitution of Kuwait requires the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> to be his descendant.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mubarak Al-Sabah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Mubarak al-Sabah</b>, who took power in 1896, signed the Anglo-Kuwaiti Agreement of 1899, placing Kuwait under British protection against Ottoman claims.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-kuwait">Sheikhdom of Kuwait</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kuwait City, in what is now <a href="/countries/kuwait">Kuwait</a>. Died in Kuwait City, in what is now <a href="/countries/kuwait">Kuwait</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.38,47.98&amp;year=1887" aria-label="Kuwait City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5800 1090 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mubarak al-Sabah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6250 1360h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6250 1360h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.38,47.98&amp;year=1887">What was here in 1887? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
